            <ArticleTitle>Bilateral mastoiditis from red tide exposure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>663-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jemermed.2010.06.007</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0736-4679(10)00517-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Bilateral mastoiditis in adults has previously been reported only in association with diabetes mellitus or immunocompromised patients.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe a case of bilateral mastoiditis in a healthy adult and to investigate the etiology.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 53-year-old woman presented to the Emergency Department with bilateral otitis externa and mastoiditis after scuba diving during a harmful algal bloom, commonly known as a &quot;red tide.&quot; The levels of coliform bacteria recorded at the time and location of her dive exceeded health regulatory limits and correlate with her atypical culture results.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Elevated bacterial counts that result from harmful algal blooms may account for this rare infection.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2012 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
